<main>

<h1>Naming outside the turn</h1>
<p class="sub">A proposal for naming new sessions and new agents without making the person wait for an extra model call on the first turn. Written 2026-10-29 from the staging measurements of the same day and the code on <code>release/v0.127.1</code>.</p>

<div class="box rec">
<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> The agent service should start one small model call at the same moment the first turn starts. The call reads the person's first message and returns a session name, a one sentence description, and, when the agent still has a placeholder name, an agent name. The service writes those names through the same endpoints the rename tools use. On that first turn, the platform instructions stop asking the model to rename anything, so the model's first call writes the reply. The rename tools stay in the tool list, so the agent can still rename itself or the session later.</p>
<p>The change is one pull request in the Python SDK (which the agent service runs), plus the benchmark fixes. It takes about 1.5 to 2 days. It costs about $0.0005 per new session with the built-in model, which is about a tenth of the extra model call it removes. In the staging runs, the first text came at 3.0 to 3.5 s when the model wrote first, against 6.6 to 9.3 s when it renamed first.</p>
</div>

<h2>The problem</h2>

<p>On the first turn of a new session, the model calls the <code>rename_session</code> tool before it writes any text in most turns. A tool call ends the model call, so the reply text needs a second model call. On staging with the built-in Gemini model (<code>google/gemini-3.8-flash</code>) and the in-process Pi harness, the model renamed first in 6 of 6 new sessions with today's instructions. The first text then came at 6.6 to 9.3 s, while turns where the model wrote first showed text at 3.0 to 3.5 s.</p>

<p>We tried to fix the order with instructions, and the best wording still renamed first in 3 of 6 sessions. New agents also start with a placeholder name ("New agent" or "Untitled agent"), so the model calls <code>rename_agent</code> on the first turn as well, with the same order problem. The names themselves were good in every run, e.g., "Billing Migration Smoke Test Plan" and "Cron vs Queue Workers".</p>

<p>The naming also costs money. Any tool call inside a turn needs one more model call after it, whatever the order. On the measured first turns, that extra call read about 4,000 new input tokens and about 20,500 cached tokens. At the built-in model's wallet price, that is about $0.006 per new session.</p>

<h2>What the code does today</h2>

<p>I read these facts from <code>origin/release/v0.127.1</code>. They decide which options are cheap.</p>

<ul>
<li>The /m app sends each turn straight to the agent service at <code>/services/agent/v0/invoke</code>. The browser never writes a session title itself.</li>
<li>The runner (the process that runs the harness and the model calls) already gives every new session a temporary title. Its first heartbeat to the API, about 0.3 s after the turn reaches it, creates the session row with the first user message, cut to 60 characters, as the name (<code>services/runner/src/sessions/name.ts</code>, <code>api/oss/src/core/sessions/streams/service.py</code>). The API only fills an empty name this way, so it never replaces a name.</li>
<li>The /m session list shows the name, or the first message preview when there is no name. The open session tab shows "New session" until a name exists. A <code>rename_session</code> call publishes a <code>session-changed</code> event, and /m refetches, so a new name shows up without a reload. A <code>rename_agent</code> call publishes <code>workflow-changed</code>, and the agent name also updates live.</li>
<li>The agent service reads the session facts before the turn starts (<code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/agents/platform/session_context.py</code>). On a first turn the row does not exist yet, so the platform instructions say "This session has no name yet. Name it with <code>rename_session</code> once the first exchange makes clear what it is about." When the agent name is a placeholder, they also say "Rename yourself with <code>rename_agent</code> in this turn." (<code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/agents/platform_instructions.py</code>, <code>session_context_guidance</code>). Each line appears only when the matching tool is in the run's tool list.</li>
<li>The default agent instructions (<code>_DEFAULT_AGENTS_MD</code> in <code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/utils/types.py</code>, with a copy in <code>services/oss/src/agent/config.py</code>) also tell the model to name the session once the first exchange is clear, and to rename itself in the same turn while its name is a placeholder. Each agent stores its own copy of these instructions when it is created, so a text change reaches new agents only.</li>
<li>On the second turn, when the agent did not rename the session, the facts read finds the runner's temporary title and the instructions say "This session is named ... Do not rename it." So a session that misses the first turn rename keeps the raw first message as its name.</li>
<li>The runner treats a change in the model's tool list between turns as a new configuration, and it opens a new harness session instead of reusing the warm one (<code>services/runner/src/lifecycle/reconciliation-router.ts</code>, "the model-visible tool catalog. Uniformly reopen in v1"). The tool list also sits early in the prompt, so a change breaks the model's prompt cache for that call.</li>
<li>The API has no helper that makes a one time model call outside an agent run, and no job that runs when a turn ends. The Python SDK already depends on <code>litellm</code>, and the agent service already holds the run's resolved model connection (provider, model, endpoint and key) in <code>handler.py</code>.</li>
<li>Price in the repo (<code>api/ee/src/core/measurements/rate_card.py</code>): the built-in <code>gemini-3.8-flash</code> costs the wallet $0.90 per million input tokens, $0.09 per million cached input tokens, and $4.50 per million output tokens. That is Google's promotional list price plus 20%. The list price doubles on 2027-01-01. There is no cheaper built-in model.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Summary of the options</h2>

<p>The running cost is per new session, with the built-in model at the wallet price. "Today" is about $0.006 per new session for the extra model call.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Option</th><th>Work</th><th>Running cost</th><th>First text</th><th>Name quality</th><th>Main risk</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A. Change the instructions only</td><td>1 h</td><td>$0.006 (same)</td><td>Fixed in 3 of 6 at best</td><td>Good</td><td>Does not meet the goal</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. Keep the first message as the name</td><td>2 h</td><td>$0</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Poor, no agent name</td><td>Raw titles</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Hide the tools on turn 1, offer them from turn 2</td><td>4 h</td><td>About $0.02 on turn 2</td><td>Fixed on turn 1, slower on turn 2</td><td>Good when it runs</td><td>Turn 2 cache miss and session reopen</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Title tag at the end of the reply</td><td>3 to 5 days</td><td>About $0.0001</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Good</td><td>Stripping a tag from streams in three harnesses</td></tr>
<tr class="pick"><td>E. Small parallel call in the agent service (recommended)</td><td>1.5 to 2 days</td><td>About $0.0005</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Good</td><td>Old agents' stored instructions</td></tr>
<tr><td>F. Small call in the API after the turn ends</td><td>3 to 4 days</td><td>About $0.001</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Good, sees the reply</td><td>New API pieces, cloud only</td></tr>
<tr><td>G. Forbid all tools on the first model call</td><td>2 h plus harness work</td><td>$0.006 (same)</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Good</td><td>Breaks first requests that need a tool</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>The options in detail</h2>

<div class="option">
<h3>A. Change the instructions only</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>The default instructions and the platform session line tell the model to write the reply first and to call the rename tools after it. The diff exists already (<code>answer-first/instruction-change.diff</code>).</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd><code>_DEFAULT_AGENTS_MD</code>, its copy in <code>services/oss/src/agent/config.py</code>, the session line in <code>platform_instructions.py</code>, two unit test asserts, and the benchmark scenario text.</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 1 hour to ship. The running cost stays at about $0.006 per new session, because the rename still needs a model call after the tool result.</dd>
<dt>Latency</dt>
<dd>Staging showed text first in 0 to 3 of 6 sessions, depending on the wording, so most first turns stay slow.</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>The model's order stays a matter of chance. Name quality, later renames, self-hosted use and the agent's own renames stay as they are today.</dd>
<dt>What it removes</dt>
<dd>Nothing.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<div class="option">
<h3>B. Keep the first message as the name, with no model</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>The runner already names each new session with the first message, cut to 60 characters. We would only remove the first turn naming lines from the platform instructions and from the default instructions. A title set by the browser would add nothing, because the runner writes the same text within the first second and /m already shows the first message as a preview.</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd><code>platform_instructions.py</code>, <code>_DEFAULT_AGENTS_MD</code> and its services copy, and the benchmark scenarios that expect a model rename.</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 2 hours. No running cost.</dd>
<dt>Latency</dt>
<dd>The first text comes from the first model call.</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>The names become raw text, e.g., "help me put together a smoke-test plan for the billing migrat". New agents keep "New agent" unless the agent renames itself on its own. Old agents' stored instructions still ask for a rename, so they still rename first. Self-hosted deployments work as well as the cloud.</dd>
<dt>What it removes</dt>
<dd>The extra model call on new agents, and the good names.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<div class="option">
<h3>C. Hide the naming tools on turn 1 and offer them from turn 2</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>The agent service drops <code>rename_session</code> and <code>rename_agent</code> from the tool list when the session facts say it is the first turn. The platform lines go away with the tools, so the model writes the reply. On turn 2 the tools come back and the model names the session.</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd>A filter on <code>resolved_tools.tool_specs</code> in <code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/agents/handler.py</code>, and a change to the turn 2 facts, so that the runner's temporary title does not trigger "Do not rename it".</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 4 hours. On turn 2, the tool list changes, so the runner opens a new harness session and the prompt cache misses. The first call of turn 2 then pays for about 20,000 more uncached tokens, about $0.016, plus the rename call itself, about $0.006.</dd>
<dt>Latency</dt>
<dd>Turn 1 is fixed. Turn 2 gets slower by the session reopen (about 0.5 s measured as cold acquire) and by the rename call when the model renames first (2.2 to 3.7 s).</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>Sessions with one turn keep the raw first message. The agent can rename itself from turn 2 only. Self-hosted deployments behave as today.</dd>
<dt>What it removes</dt>
<dd>The delay on turn 1 only. It moves the cost to turn 2 and adds a cache miss.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<div class="option">
<h3>D. The model appends a title tag that the runner strips</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>The instructions ask the model to end its first reply with a tag such as <code>&lt;session-title&gt;...&lt;/session-title&gt;</code>. The runner removes the tag from the text stream before it reaches the client and writes the name.</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd>The runner's stream code for each harness (Pi, Claude Code and Codex), the stored history and traces so the tag does not leak, the instructions, and the benchmark.</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 3 to 5 days, because the tag can split across stream chunks and each harness streams differently. The running cost is about 20 output tokens, about $0.0001.</dd>
<dt>Latency</dt>
<dd>No extra call. The end of the reply is a fraction of a second later.</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>A tag that the runner misses shows up to the person. The model may copy the tag into later replies once it sees it in history. The runner and the agent service ship on separate schedules, so a version mismatch can show raw tags. Custom instructions can override the tag rule.</dd>
<dt>What it removes</dt>
<dd>The extra model call. It adds a new parsing path that every harness must keep.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<div class="option pick">
<h3>E. A small parallel model call in the agent service <span class="tag good">recommended</span></h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>When the session facts say it is the first turn and the session has no name, the agent service starts a second, small model call next to the turn. The call uses the run's own model connection and sees only the first user message, and it returns a session name, a description and, when the agent name is a placeholder, an agent name. The service writes them with the same endpoints the rename tools call, with <code>name_source=automatic</code>, so a name a person typed always wins. On that turn the platform session line says the platform names the session and the agent, and asks the model not to call the rename tools. The tools stay in the list, so the tool list does not change between turns.</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd>A new module <code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/agents/naming.py</code>, the start and the wait in <code>handler.py</code>, a flag into <code>session_context_guidance</code> in <code>platform_instructions.py</code>, the naming bullet in <code>_DEFAULT_AGENTS_MD</code> and its services copy, unit tests, and the benchmark.</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 1.5 to 2 days. The call reads about 300 input tokens and writes about 40 output tokens with thinking turned off, about $0.0005 per new session on the built-in model, or $0.50 per 1,000 new sessions. Today's extra call costs about $0.006, so the change saves about $5 per 1,000 new sessions.</dd>
<dt>Latency</dt>
<dd>The first text comes from the first model call, at 3.0 to 3.5 s in the staging runs. The small call runs at the same time as the turn, so the name should appear in about 2 to 3 s, often before the reply. The runner's temporary title shows until then.</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>
<ul>
<li>Name quality. The call sees the first message and not the reply. The good staging names all came from the first message, so I expect similar quality. A very short message such as "hi" gives a weak name. In that case the call should return nothing, and the temporary title stays.</li>
<li>Old agents. Agents created before the change keep their stored naming bullet. The platform line tells the model not to rename on the first turn, but that line conflicts with their stored text, and staging showed that instructions alone are unreliable. The timing check must measure old and new instructions separately. If old agents still rename first, the fallback is to hide the two tools on the first turn for agents with the old bullet only, which costs them the turn 2 reopen from option C.</li>
<li>Later renames. They stay as they are today. The agent can still call <code>rename_session</code> when the topic changes and <code>rename_agent</code> when a person asks. The existing gap stays, because Gemini did not rename on a topic change in the benchmark (name-02 failed 3 of 3).</li>
<li>Self-hosted. The call uses the run's own connection through <code>litellm</code>, so it works with a person's own provider key, and it costs a few hundred tokens on that key. When the connection has no key the service can use, such as a subscription login, the service skips the call and keeps today's instructions, so those agents name themselves as today.</li>
<li>Timing of the write. A header write on a session that does not exist creates the row, which must not happen before the runner creates it with the right owner and access. The service should write only after the first runner frame arrives.</li>
</ul>
</dd>
<dt>What it removes</dt>
<dd>The extra model call on almost every first turn, and the first turn naming lines from the instructions. It keeps the rename tools and the good names.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<div class="option">
<h3>F. A small model call in the API after the turn ends</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>When the runner's final heartbeat says the turn stopped, the API enqueues a job. A worker reads the first exchange, makes a small call through the built-in model gateway, and writes the names.</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd>A new one time completion helper over <code>LLMService.relay_chat_completion</code> in <code>api/oss/src/core/gateways/llms/service.py</code>, a job at the end of the turn in <code>api/oss/src/core/sessions/streams/service.py</code>, a new worker, and the same instruction and benchmark changes as option E.</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 3 to 4 days. The call also reads the start of the reply, about 1,000 input tokens, so about $0.001 per new session.</dd>
<dt>Latency</dt>
<dd>The first text is fixed. The name appears only after the whole reply ends, which can be a minute for long agent turns.</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>The built-in gateway exists only in the cloud, so self-hosted deployments need another path. The API would gain its first server side model call, with its own wallet charge and its own failure handling.</dd>
<dt>What it removes</dt>
<dd>The same as option E, with more new code in the API.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<div class="option">
<h3>G. Forbid all tools on the first model call</h3>
<dl>
<dt>How it works</dt>
<dd>The harness sends the first model call of a new session with tools turned off, so the model must write text.</dd>
<dt>What changes</dt>
<dd>The runner and each harness adapter.</dd>
<dt>Cost</dt>
<dd>About 2 hours for Pi, but Claude Code and Codex do not expose this setting. The running cost stays the same, because the rename call still follows.</dd>
<dt>Risks</dt>
<dd>A first request that needs a tool, e.g., "read this file", would get a reply that guesses before it reads. I reject this option.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

<h2>Why option E</h2>

<p>Option E removes the extra model call for every new session whose connection has a usable key, and it keeps the names as good as today. It needs no change in the runner, so the runner and the agent service can ship on their own schedules without a mismatch. It keeps the tool list the same on every turn, so turn 2 keeps its warm session and its prompt cache, which option C loses. It costs about a tenth of today's naming, and it is cheaper to build than options D and F because the agent service already holds the model connection, the session facts and the API credentials.</p>

<h2>Steps for one small PR</h2>

<ol>
<li>Add <code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/agents/naming.py</code> with one function that takes the resolved connection, the first user message (cut to about 2,000 characters) and whether the agent name is a placeholder. It calls <code>litellm.acompletion</code> with a short prompt, a 60 token output limit, thinking turned off and a 10 s timeout. It returns a session name of at most 60 characters, a description, and an optional agent name, or nothing when the message is too vague. Check that the built-in gateway passes the setting that turns thinking off.</li>
<li>In <code>sdks/python/agenta/sdk/agents/handler.py</code>, after the session facts resolve, start the call as a task when <code>first_turn</code> is true, the session has no name, and the connection has a key the service can use. Write the names after the first runner frame, with <code>PUT /api/sessions/streams/header?name_source=automatic</code> and, for a placeholder agent name, <code>PUT /api/workflows/{id}</code> with the name only. Keep the task alive until the stream ends, with a time limit, and log failures without showing them to the person.</li>
<li>In <code>platform_instructions.py</code>, add a flag to <code>session_context_guidance</code>. When the platform names the session, the first turn line says the platform names the session and the agent and asks the model not to call the rename tools in this turn. Without the flag, the line stays as today.</li>
<li>In <code>_DEFAULT_AGENTS_MD</code> and <code>services/oss/src/agent/config.py</code>, replace the first exchange naming bullet with "call <code>rename_session</code> only when the topic changes" and "call <code>rename_agent</code> when your purpose changes or a person asks".</li>
<li>Fix the self_naming benchmark (<code>docs/design/agent-config-editing/benchmark/</code>). Set <code>REPO_ROOT</code> to <code>parents[4]</code> in <code>bench_lib.py</code> (line 60). Add <code>"permission": "allow"</code> to the rename tool entries in name-01 to name-05 (lines 22, 68, 109, 168 and 202 of <code>09-self-naming.json</code>), because today only name-06 sets it and the other renames stop at an approval gate. Change name-01, name-03, name-05 and name-06 to check the stored names and that no rename tool ran before the first text. Keep name-02 (topic change) and name-04 (no rename on a named session) as they are.</li>
<li>Add unit tests for the naming module (parsing, length limits, vague message), for the handler (the call runs on the first turn only, never with a subscription login, never over a named session), and for the new instruction line.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How to test it</h2>

<ol>
<li>Run the SDK and services unit tests with <code>py-run-tests</code> in <code>sdks/python</code> and <code>services</code>.</li>
<li>Deploy to GKE staging and run the first turn timing probe (<code>answer-first/firstturn.py</code>) with built-in Gemini and the in-process Pi harness, with 6 new sessions on new agents and 6 new sessions on an agent that keeps the old stored instructions. Pass means the first text comes from the first model call in at least 5 of 6 sessions in each group, the median first text is about 3.5 s or less in a calm Vertex period, and the session name and agent name are set within 5 s of the send.</li>
<li>Run the fixed self_naming benchmark on the built-in Gemini cell, on an organization with enough credits. The last run ran out of credits after 17 turns and left 12 of 18 trials unsettled.</li>
<li>Open /m on staging, start a new session on a new agent, and check that the tab and the list change from the first message to the new name without a reload, and that the agent name changes from "New agent".</li>
